  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1208 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nA split flow pattern in the upper troposphere heading into the \nlatter part of the week will maintain steady and generally \nuneventful weather. Southerly flow will push the cooler airmass away \nwith weak warm advection arriving from Mexico. Earlier indications \nthat overnight lows could quickly become noticeably mild this week \nhave eased with models trending towards keeping more moisture over \nthe Gulf. However, we do still expect an uptrend in temperatures as \nthe a polar jet streak progresses over the central Rockies and \nnorthern Plains, increasing warm air advection. Temperatures are \nprojected to rise to near or slightly above mid November averages.\n\nA weak cold front is forecast to sag near our area this weekend. The \ntrailing airmass does not look markedly cold and the shepherding \ntrough is expected to well to our north, so the progression of the \nfront will likely be slow barring a notable change in the forecast \ntrough position. If the front stays somewhat stationary over South \nCentral Texas, as most models indicate, then the ascent of moist air \nover the boundary encouraged by the subtropical jet aloft provides a \nwindow for rain chances late Saturday into early next week. Without \na distinct disturbance in the subtropical jet to focus activity, \nthis could be a period of broad showery activity, or a few rounds of \nrainfall, similar to this past weekend across South Central Texas. \nRain chances during this period range from 30 to 50 percent. \nEnsemble sensitivity analyses indicate the amount and likelihood of \nrainfall will be dependent on the placement of the aforementioned \ncold front, which may not be well established until we get a good \nread on the trough moving over the central Rockies on Friday.",
